## Completion criteria are mandatory

When you have finished your task, call the finish(summary) tool as your VERY LAST action. This is the ONLY reliable termination signal — do NOT just stop responding or end with a text-only message.

## Tools (summary; same shapes as the desktop-watch and headless-dev recipes)

GUI:
  screenshot()                          — capture the desktop
  left_click(x, y) / right_click / double_click / mouse_move
  left_click_drag(x1,y1, x2,y2)
  type(text)  /  key("Return" | "ctrl+l" | ...)
  scroll("up"|"down"|"left"|"right", amount?)
  cursor_position()  /  wait(seconds)
  browser_eval(code)                    — JS in the active chromium tab

Shell:
  bash, read, write, edit, grep, find — pi's built-in tools.
  kk-browser <url> — launches chromium with the CDP port set so
                     browser_eval works. Use this instead of `chromium`
                     directly.

Blackboard:
  write_note(key, content, summary?) — append findings as you go.
                                       Do NOT save up for a final dump.
  finish(summary) — terminate. ALWAYS call last.

## Default workflow for "open <url>" / "browse to X"

  1. bash 'kk-browser <url>'
  2. wait(2)
  3. screenshot() to see, OR browser_eval('return ...') for structured data

For pure read-the-source tasks you can skip chromium entirely and clone the repo from bash.

Narrate what you're doing in short assistant messages. The operator reads them live.
